## 1.5.1 — 2026-09-05

Scope the 1.5.0 reference remedies to the decisions their evidence supports,
so a future campaign can choose a valid experiment or representation.

Compile-cost guidance now asks which work a change removes and what a
parallelism knob controls. Flag syntax probes and kernel lowering probes can
reuse matching evidence. Counts keep their emitter, stage, and normalizer;
an arithmetic cross-check now needs a known relation. Timeout budgets follow
the actual waits and clock origins instead of a fixed ordering.

Device-free probes match production context when they claim production
compatibility. Isolated probes state their narrower claim. Clamp and sentinel
remedies preserve the producer's range and bit meaning. Low-bit scale rounding
follows the format, and the boundary-count lookup remedy applies to ordered
boundaries. These replace unconditional recipes in the existing references.

The defect review found that two remedies from a sibling port campaign had
become universal instructions. It did not refute the compiler observations or
supply new campaign measurements. Prior usage summaries in releases 1.2.0–1.5.0
and ledger revisions 1–5 argue against repeat work on settled inputs. This
release adds no seat, lap, artifact, or recurring gate. It preserves revision
5's acceptance controls, scan evidence, and measured hardware refusals.

The graph, ledger, state authority, hooks, and model bindings do not change.
Both plugin manifests declare 1.5.1. Historical entries below describe their
own releases; their remedy and learning counts are not current policy.

## 1.5.0 — 2026-09-04

Field learnings from a finished port campaign of the retiring sibling workflow
(`vllm-neuron-model-port`, one model family on trn2 at Neuron SDK 2.32 and
vllm-neuron 0.24) land in the reference layer. Read them for what they are: the
same hardware class and the same stack as a parity run, measured on a different
workflow's campaign, so each rule is a fact about the toolchain and the fork at
that pin and none of them is parity evidence. The graph's meaning does not move
in this release. The three acceptance-instrument duties that DO need the graph
land separately as ledger revision 5 (32 nodes and 95 edges unchanged, checks 15
to 16); this release carries the shapes that revision cites, so the two belong
to one landing.

Provenance of every rule below: 713 raw candidates from that campaign,
consolidated to 342, then three filters (drop what public documentation already
states with a quotable URL, drop what an installed Neuron skill already states,
drop what is not still true at SDK 2.32 / vllm-neuron 0.24) and two refuters
(truth, value). 172 survived. 42 more were not verifiable at this pin and 66
stayed contested; both sets are deliberately absent — an unproven rule in a
package whose whole discipline is provenance is worse than no rule.

Every carried learning is cited where its mechanism landed: all 172, none
uncited — 171 by id in the four references below, 6 in ledger revision 5's
semantic diff, union 172. They collapse to 29 rules in this release layer,
beside the 3 duties in ledger revision 5 — 32 across both layers. The basis for
29, each component greppable: 13 `**Rule:**` blocks in
`references/toolchain-evidence-pitfalls.md`, 8 added `**Rule` lines in
`references/measurement-pitfalls.md` (12 to 20), 4 named duty blocks in
`references/artifact-layout.md` (instrument-liveness pair, scan-completeness
discipline, evaluated-threshold record, instances on this hardware class), 3
items in the new section of `references/patch-mechanism-inventory.md`, and 1 new
refusal class in the compile-cache guard. §4.13 is excluded: it is a move, not a
new rule. Clustering by mechanism is the point — one mechanism, one rule, every
id that found it. Read the 172 as CITATION coverage, not duty coverage: review sampled five landed ids and found
two whose duty was partial or missing behind a correct citation (a one-sided
clamp bound, and a segmented-prefill precondition cited but never stated), both
repaired before landing. A citation proves provenance; only reading the rule
proves the duty arrived.

New — `references/toolchain-evidence-pitfalls.md` (372 lines, ceiling 400): 13
rules for reading what the Neuron compiler and runtime say about themselves.
Re-derive every second-hand claim at the pin; a flag's behaviour lives in the
installed binary; credit a flag only after you prove delivery and that the pass
ran; compile cost is composition, not graph size; price a device-memory
overflow from the graph's own deduplicated resident I/O; a compiler-internal
name or id is an anonymous version-minted lead, never a join key; each compiler
artifact owns a disjoint field set and its own survival rule; per-core artifacts
are legitimately asymmetric at two logical cores per device; a kernel is opaque
to every graph-level channel; every bound in the stack is late and bounds a
different wait; a runtime knob is delivered only when the runtime's own render
changes; live wedge state is per-core and reachable only through the driver; the
pipeline aborts at its first unmet precondition, so a cleared wall buys the next
stage and nothing more.
  - Why a new file: about 94 of the carried learnings had no home. The existing
    four references are the instruments that produce a number for a verdict
    (`measurement-pitfalls.md`), the fork's patch surfaces
    (`patch-mechanism-inventory.md`), the file surfaces where ports collide
    (`collision-ranking.md`), and the run's own artifact tree
    (`artifact-layout.md`). A reader of all four would still credit a compiler
    flag from its name, join compiler artifacts by an internal id, read a field
    from the wrong compiler file, and reset a shared device on a late watchdog
    status.
  - Why one file and not two: the compiler half and the device half are read by
    the same two seats (implementer at an attempt, investigator at costing and
    triage) at overlapping moments, and they share one mechanism — the channel
    reports a position, a name, or a count for its own purposes. Two authorities
    for one duty drift apart.
  - Each rule carries a one-sentence reason beside the trap, so a reader can
    judge a case the rule did not foresee. That is the anti-patch instrument: it
    is what keeps a rule from becoming a fix shaped to one incident.

Changed — `references/measurement-pitfalls.md` (104 to 158 lines, ceiling 200):
three new rules and two extensions. Prove the instrument before its verdict
counts (a known-positive and a known-negative per criterion; an exit status is
not an evaluation; a criterion whose instrument cannot be made to fail is
unadjudicable). A zero is evidence only with a firing control and a bogus
control on the same artifact type at the same verbosity. Record the emitter, the
stage, and the divisor with every count, and confirm a count's role against a
second independently printed quantity. A completion signal reports a position,
not completion. Size host memory in the deployed multi-rank context, never from
a proxy. Capture the stream the component actually writes. The compile-cache
rule gains which fix site to pick: the key hashes graph structure, so a
producer-side fix keeps the warm cache and a zero compiled-graph count on an
expected-warm start proves it — with the pin caveat that the 0.24 key list drops
the fork's port-added FX-pass fingerprint and the compile path moved into
`libtorch_neuronx_lite`, so re-derive both the component list and the counter
name at your own pin.

Changed — `references/artifact-layout.md` (399 to 477 lines, ceiling 500). §4.5
gains the instrument-liveness pair the graph now registers per criterion (the
value that must appear as evaluated, and the tripwire input the procedure must
fail on). §4.6 gains the scan-completeness discipline (a reported count carries
the commit scanned, the tree state, and the tool's own completion signal; a tool
that gates on a clean tree runs in a fresh throwaway worktree) and the
evaluated-threshold record every evidence bundle carries. §4.10 gains the four
measured instances of durable host state. §4.13 is new: the write-for-reader
duty, defined once for every seat.

Changed — `references/patch-mechanism-inventory.md` (138 to 148 lines, ceiling
180): one new section for the 0.24 line and SDK 2.32, with its own pin note
because every section above it is the 0.21 checkout. Upstream decides at a
consumer you did not patch. Import time pins the venue before your code runs —
so a device-free probe is constructed the way the runner constructs the model,
or it grades a configuration the port never runs. The front end's authoring
constraints: an in-range clamp maximum, a signed sentinel, an exactly floored
low-bit scale, no literal constant tensor inside a traced forward pass, and a
checkpoint resolved from its files rather than its names.

Changed — `skills/vllm-neuron-parity/SKILL.md` (472 to 460 lines, ceiling 500).
The 25-line write-for-reader restatement becomes a 13-line pointer at
`references/artifact-layout.md` §4.13 and §4.12, which is where the duty and the
cap now live once. The brief list gains the new reference; the stage-7 row briefs
attempt and triage seats with it; the stage-8 row states the instrument-liveness
duty the graph carries.

Changed — `README.md` (642 to 652 lines, ceiling 700) and the lead skill drop
every hard-coded ledger-head number. The appendix, the file tree, the migration
steps, and the skill's evolution-root paragraph now name "the ledger head" and
send the reader to `revisions.yaml` and the validator. Reason: those numbers
were stale the moment a revision landed, and a rendered view that states a fact
the authority owns is a second authority. Dated changelog entries keep their
numbers — 1.4.0 shipped revision 4 with 15 checks, and that stays true of 1.4.0.

Changed — four seat contracts gain one pointer clause each, identical in both
harness ports (`agents/*.md` and `codex/agents/*.toml`). The implementer's
increment plan declares the venue its CPU-mode acceptance grades, and its
attempt loop reads the toolchain reference before attributing a failure. The
investigator gets compile costing and the second-hand-claim duty. The measurer
gets instrument liveness and the firing control. The adjudicator takes each
measured value from the bundle's own evaluated-threshold record and never from a
comparator's exit status. No duty text is restated in a contract: every clause
points at the one authority.

Changed — `skills/vllm-neuron-parity/hooks/compile-cache-guard.sh` refuses
irreversible verbs (`rm`, `rmdir`, `unlink`, `shred`, `truncate`, `trash`,
`find -delete`, `git clean`, `rsync --delete`) against
`/var/tmp/nki-intermediate-cache`, where the kernel toolchain writes its
compiled kernel artifacts — outside every cache root a run can set, and possibly
holding a co-tenant's. `mv` stays allowed there on purpose: renaming aside is
the sanctioned clear, and a delete is the irreversible act. The guard still
registers as one of eight controls.
  - What the prohibition always said, and what the prose got wrong: the graph
    declares this forbidden effect as a CLASS — "clearing the shared compile
    cache", at `realize_increment`, `execute_attempt_loop`, and
    `recover_leased_host` — enumerating no paths and carrying no `P2` label. The
    enumeration lived only in package prose, and it named three of the four
    roots in that class. This release corrects an incomplete enumeration; it
    does not widen a prohibition, and no graph text moves. The fourth root is in
    the class on the facts: the kernel toolchain compiles into it, it is shared
    between tenants, and the same measured remedy ("clear the cache") aims at
    it.
  - Every copy of the enumeration is corrected: the P2 row and the hook
    disclosure in `skills/vllm-neuron-parity/SKILL.md`, the prohibition row in
    `README.md`, the P2 clause in all six seat contracts, their six codex
    mirrors, and the guard's own header and refusal text.
    `references/artifact-layout.md` §4.10 holds the instances and the
    rename-aside duty once.
  - Field evidence: that directory holds the real kernel artifacts and can hold
    a co-tenant's, so a documented "clear the cache" remedy followed there
    destroys another tenant's work.

New — `tests/test_document_ceilings.py`: every prose document in the package
sits under a pinned line ceiling, and every prose document is pinned. A release
that grows a document past its ceiling raises the ceiling in the same change,
with the reason here. This is the ratchet the package did not have; the prose
budget was a promise before it.

Not landed, deliberately. The digest's second proposed new reference (a separate
runtime-hang-triage file) — folded into the one file above, same reason as
"why one file". `references/collision-ranking.md` untouched: the seven rows
mapped to it are compile-cost and upstream-gating facts, and its subject is
